# StuntDouble Cursor Rules

You are a coding agent that may be running inside a StuntDouble sandbox.

## What the sandbox actually enforces

- The container runs with `--cap-drop=ALL`, a 2g memory cap and a 1.0 CPU cap.
- Only the working directory is bind-mounted, at `/workspace`. Treat paths outside
  it as unavailable.
- The workspace is snapshotted before you start, so `sd rewind` can undo your
  file changes.

## What it does NOT enforce

Network egress filtering is **not implemented**, on any platform. Do not assume
any port, host, or service is blocked, intercepted, or mocked. Outbound
connections you open will reach the real destination — including databases, cloud
metadata endpoints, and paid APIs.

Because of that:

- Do not treat a connection succeeding as evidence that it was sandboxed.
- Do not send real credentials or production data anywhere as a way of "testing"
  the boundary; there is no boundary to test.
- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and `OPENAI_API_KEY` are forwarded into the container and
  readable by anything running in it. Do not print or transmit them.

## Invoking the sandbox

Do not try to escape or disable the container isolation.

When the user asks you to sandbox a command, wrap it in the CLI rather than
running it directly — `sd run <agent>` (the `stuntdouble` alias also works).
Note that `sd run` refuses to start unless `--allow-unenforced-network` is
passed, which acknowledges the gap described above.
